The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (optlen && ip_options_compile(net, &opt->opt, NULL)) {
kfree(*optp);
*optp = opt;
Reason:
After parsing the options and setting the `srr` flag via `ip_options_compile`, the function stores the options unconditionally. No capability check is performed, so an unprivileged user can set source routing options (IPOPT_SSRR / IPOPT_LSRR), which can be exploited to leak TCP ISN or route packets through attacker-controlled nodes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (opt->opt.srr && !ns_capable(net->user_ns, CAP_NET_RAW)) {
    kfree(opt);
    return -EPERM;
}
Reason:
The added condition checks if the options contain a source route (`srr` flag set) and if the caller lacks `CAP_NET_RAW` in the network namespace. If both hold, the function frees the allocated option structure and returns `-EPERM`, preventing unprivileged applications from setting source routing options.

